window.__finlexDocLoaded(10073,{"id":10073,"articles":[{"id":1000243,"no":"Overview","heading":"Official Overview & Scope (IFRS Foundation)","text":"IFRS 16 is effective for annual reporting periods beginning on or after 1 January 2019, with earlier application permitted (as long as IFRS 15 is also applied).\n\nThe objective of IFRS 16 is to report information that (a) faithfully represents lease transactions and (b) provides a basis for users of financial statements to assess the amount, timing and uncertainty of cash flows arising from leases. To meet that objective, a lessee should recognise assets and liabilities arising from a lease.\n\nIFRS 16 introduces a single lessee accounting model and requires a lessee to recognise assets and liabilities for all leases with a term of more than 12 months, unless the underlying asset is of low value. A lessee is required to recognise a right-of-use asset representing its right to use the underlying leased asset and a lease liability representing its obligation to make lease payments.","seq":0},{"id":1000244,"no":"IFRS 16.9–21","heading":"Identifying a Lease, Separating Components & Lease Term","text":"A contract is (or contains) a lease if it conveys the right to control the use of an identified asset for a period of time in exchange for consideration (IFRS 16.9, B9–B31).\n\n[Control Test & Lease Term]\n• Control requires BOTH: (a) right to obtain substantially all economic benefits from use of the identified asset, and (b) right to direct the use of the identified asset (no substantive substitution right by supplier, IFRS 16.B14–B19).\n• Separating components (IFRS 16.12–16): lessees allocate consideration to lease and non-lease components based on relative stand-alone prices, unless electing the practical expedient by class of underlying asset to account for them as a single lease component (IFRS 16.15).\n• Lease term (IFRS 16.18–21): non-cancellable period plus periods covered by an option to extend (if reasonably certain to exercise) or an option to terminate (if reasonably certain NOT to exercise).","seq":1},{"id":1000245,"no":"IFRS 16.22–46","heading":"Lessee Accounting: ROU Asset, Lease Liability & Modifications","text":"Single on-balance-sheet lessee model (IFRS 16.22): recognise a right-of-use (ROU) asset and a lease liability at commencement date.\n\n[Recognition Exemptions (IFRS 16.5–8)]\n• Short-term leases (lease term ≤ 12 months with no purchase option, by asset class) and leases of low-value assets (e.g., tablets, small IT equipment, on a lease-by-lease basis) may be expensed straight-line.\n\n[Measurement & Subsequent Accounting]\n• Lease liability (IFRS 16.26–28): present value of unpaid lease payments (fixed payments, index/rate-based variable payments, residual value guarantees, reasonably certain purchase/termination options) discounted at the interest rate implicit in the lease or lessee's incremental borrowing rate (IBR).\n• ROU asset (IFRS 16.23–25): initial lease liability + prepayments + initial direct costs + estimated restoration costs (IAS 37) − lease incentives received; depreciated under IAS 16 and tested for impairment under IAS 36.\n• Lease modifications (IFRS 16.44–46): separate lease if adding right to use one or more assets at stand-alone price; otherwise remeasure lease liability with revised discount rate and adjust ROU asset (recognising P&L gain/loss for scope decreases).","seq":2},{"id":1000246,"no":"IFRS 16.61–103","heading":"Lessor Accounting & Sale-and-Leaseback Transactions","text":"• Dual lessor model (IFRS 16.61–66): classify each lease as a finance lease (transfers substantially all risks and rewards incidental to ownership) or an operating lease.\n• Finance lessor (IFRS 16.67–78): derecognise underlying asset, recognise net investment in the lease receivable, and recognise finance income over the lease term.\n• Operating lessor (IFRS 16.81–87): keep asset on balance sheet and recognise lease income on a straight-line (or other systematic) basis.\n• Sale and leaseback (IFRS 16.98–103): apply IFRS 15 to test whether transfer is a sale. If a sale, seller-lessee measures ROU asset at the proportion of previous carrying amount retained and recognises gain/loss ONLY for rights transferred to the buyer-lessor (amended 2022 for variable lease payments in 16.102A). If not a sale, account as a financial liability under IFRS 9.","seq":3},{"id":1000247,"no":"CAS 21 vs IFRS 16","heading":"Cross-Framework Practical Comparison (China CAS 21 vs. IFRS 16)","text":"China CAS 21 (2018 Revision) is closely converged with IFRS 16 (single lessee balance-sheet model, dual lessor model, and IFRS 15-linked sale-and-leaseback test).\n• Low-value asset threshold: IFRS 16 Basis for Conclusions cites USD 5,000 as a reference benchmark; China CAS 21 Application Guidance commonly references RMB 40,000 for brand-new underlying assets.\n• Variable leaseback payments: IFRS 16.102A (effective 1 Jan 2024) explicitly prevents seller-lessees from recognising gains on retained rights for variable lease payments.","seq":4},{"id":1000248,"no":"History 1","heading":"Standard History & Amendments","text":"In April 2001 the International Accounting Standards Board (Board) adopted IAS 17 Leases, which had originally been issued by the International Accounting Standards Committee (IASC) in December 1997. IAS 17 Leases replaced IAS 17 Accounting for Leases that was issued in September 1982.\n\nIn April 2001 the Board adopted SIC‑15 Operating Leases—Incentives, which had originally been issued by the Standing Interpretations Committee of the IASC in December 1998.\n\nIn December 2001 the Board issued SIC‑27 Evaluating the Substance of Transactions Involving the Legal Form of a Lease. SIC‑27 had originally been developed by the Standing Interpretations Committee of the IASC to provide guidance on determining, amongst other things, whether an arrangement that involves the legal form of a lease meets the definition of a lease under IAS 17.","seq":5},{"id":1000249,"no":"History 2","heading":"Standard History & Amendments","text":"In December 2003 the Board issued a revised IAS 17 as part of its initial agenda of technical projects.\n\nIn December 2004 the Board issued IFRIC 4 Determining whether an Arrangement contains a Lease. The Interpretation was developed by the Interpretations Committee to provide guidance on determining whether transactions that do not take the legal form of a lease but convey the right to use an asset in return for a payment or series of payments are, or contain, leases that should be accounted for in accordance with IAS 17.\n\nIn January 2016 the Board issued IFRS 16 Leases. IFRS 16 replaces IAS 17, IFRIC 4, SIC‑15 and SIC‑27. IFRS 16 sets out the principles for the recognition, measurement, presentation and disclosure of leases.","seq":6},{"id":1000250,"no":"History 3","heading":"Standard History & Amendments","text":"In May 2020 the Board issued Covid-19-Related Rent Concessions, which amended IFRS 16. The amendment permits lessees, as a practical expedient, not to assess whether rent concessions that occur as a direct consequence of the covid-19 pandemic and meet specified conditions are lease modifications. Instead, the lessee accounts for those rent concessions as if they were not lease modifications.\n\nIn August 2020 the Board issued Interest Rate Benchmark Reform―Phase 2 which amended requirements in IFRS 9, IAS 39, IFRS 7, IFRS 4 and IFRS 16 relating to:\n\nchanges in the basis for determining contractual cash flows of financial assets, financial liabilities and lease liabilities;","seq":7},{"id":1000251,"no":"History 4","heading":"Standard History & Amendments","text":"hedge accounting; and\n\ndisclosures.\n\nThe Phase 2 amendments apply only to changes required by the interest rate benchmark reform to financial instruments and hedging relationships.","seq":8},{"id":1000252,"no":"History 5","heading":"Standard History & Amendments","text":"In September 2022, the Board issued Lease Liability in a Sale and Leaseback, which added subsequent measurement requirements for sale and leaseback transactions.\n\nOther Standards have made minor consequential amendments to IFRS 16, including Amendments to References to the Conceptual Framework in IFRS Standards (issued March 2018) and IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements (issued April 2024).","seq":9},{"id":1000253,"no":"Support","heading":"Related Projects & Implementation Support","text":"[Related active projects]\n• Leases—Targeted Improvements (Amendments to IFRS 16)\n• Sale and Leaseback of an Asset in a Single-Asset Entity (IFRS 10 and IFRS 16)\n\n[Related completed projects]\n• Definition of a Lease—Shipping Contract (IFRS 16 Leases)\n• Definition of a Lease—Substitution Rights (IFRS 16)\n• Economic Benefits from Use of a Battery under an Offtake Arrangement (IFRS 16)\n• Economic Benefits from Use of a Windfarm (IFRS 16)\n• IBOR Reform and its Effects on Financial Reporting—Phase 2\n• IFRS 16 and covid-19\n• IFRS Accounting Taxonomy Update—Amendments to IFRS 16 and IAS 1\n• IFRS Taxonomy Update—Covid-19-Related Rent Concessions (Amendment to IFRS 16)\n• IFRS Taxonomy Update—Interest Rate Benchmark Reform—Phase 2\n• Lease Incentives (Amendment to Illustrative Example 13 accompanying IFRS 16)\n• Lease Liability in a Sale and Leaseback\n• Lease Term and Useful Life of Leasehold Improvements (IFRS 16 Leases and IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment)\n• Leases\n• Lessee’s Incremental Borrowing Rate (IFRS 16 Leases)\n• Lessor Forgiveness of Lease Payments (IFRS 9 and IFRS 16)\n• Non-refundable Value Added Tax on Lease Payments (IFRS 16)\n• Post-implementation Review of IFRS 16 Leases\n• Sale and Leaseback with Variable Payments (IFRS 16)\n• Subsurface Rights (IFRS 16)\n• Video: Tadeu Cendon explains the Request for Information\n\n[Related IFRS Standards]\n• IAS 17 Leases\n• IAS 36 Impairment of Assets\n\n[Unconsolidated amendments]\n• Обязательство по аренде в операции продажи с обратной арендой Поправки к МСФО (IFRS) 16","seq":10},{"id":1000254,"no":"References","heading":"Official Text Link & Public Practical Guidance","text":"Official IFRS Foundation Page (English): https://www.ifrs.org/issued-standards/list-of-standards/ifrs-16-leases/\nAccess Note: Free access to the unaccompanied text of IFRS Accounting Standards on ifrs.org requires a free registered IFRS.org account; Basis for Conclusions and Illustrative Examples require an IFRS Digital Subscription.\nPublic Companion Overview: KPMG Insights into IFRS — An Overview (Sept 2026): https://assets.kpmg.com/content/dam/kpmgsites/xx/pdf/ifrg/2026/isg-2026-insights-an-overview.pdf\nKPMG IFRS Accounting Toolkit (Standard Handbooks): https://kpmg.com/xx/en/what-we-do/services/audit/corporate-reporting-institute/ifrs/toolkit.html","seq":11}]});
